# Code Review — UIABLE Quality Gate

Mandatory review for every component entering UIABLE-INTERNAL, newly authored or migrated from Berry, Mantis, or Saascable. **This is a blocking gate** — code is not finalized until every applicable check passes.

---

## How to Run

Three passes, cheapest first. **Stop and fix at the first failing pass** — do not review for style while the machine checks are still red.

| Pass             | What it covers                                                         | Cost               |
| :--------------- | :--------------------------------------------------------------------- | :----------------- |
| 1 — Machine      | §1.3–§1.5, §2.2, §2.4, §2.6–§2.7, §2.10, §3.13, §4.2, §4.4, §5.4, §9.7, §12.1–12.3, §12.5–12.8 | one command batch  |
| 2 — Read-through | §1–§11, §14                                                            | one read of a file |
| 3 — Registry     | §13 (blocks only)                                                      | two file checks    |
| 4 — Judgment     | anything the numbered checks do not name                               | one look at the whole block |

Mark every check ✅ PASS / ❌ FAIL / ⏭️ N/A on each iteration of the **Convergence Loop** below, fixing failures as you go. Only emit the report — using the **Review Summary Template** at the end of this file — once the loop converges.

> **Checks marked (CRITICAL) are hard blockers.** A single violation means the component is not ready, no matter how clean the rest of the review is. They cannot be marked ⏭️ N/A, cannot be waived as a follow-up, and cannot be traded against a passing verdict elsewhere. Fix them before the review is emitted.
>
> Currently critical: **2.6** (gap over margin/padding), **3.7** (named interface above 2 props), **3.9** (extract reusable sub-components), **3.13** (extract inline SVG).
>
> **⚠️ 2.6 is the first among these.** It is the most-violated rule in the checklist and the one that fails most quietly — margin-based spacing renders identically to gap-based spacing, so nothing about the output betrays it. The Pass 1 grep only finds containers where margin is *already present*; a multi-child container with no margin and no `gap` is equally a violation and is invisible to it. Two things close that hole, and both are required: the Pass 2 instruction to check **every** container with ≥2 children, and the DOM assertion at `block-audit` **4.9**, which proves it against computed styles rather than class strings.

### Convergence Loop

Passes 1–3 are **one iteration**, not a one-shot script. Run the full cycle, fix every failure it finds, then run the full cycle again from Pass 1. Repeat until one complete iteration turns up **zero** ❌ across every applicable check.

```text
iteration:
  run Pass 1 → Pass 2 → Pass 3 → Pass 4
  if any check is ❌:
    fix it
    iteration += 1
    go to iteration          # restart from Pass 1, not from where the fix was
  else:
    stop — emit the summary
```

**Why restart from Pass 1, not resume where the fix landed.** A fix routinely creates or exposes a violation outside the section it touched:

- Extracting a sub-component per **3.9** can push it past two props, triggering **3.7**
- Replacing a hardcoded hex per **2.4** can change how a card reads in dark mode, triggering **6.1**
- Deleting a JSX comment per **12.8** can leave a stray blank line, triggering **12.6**
- Renaming a helper to a `function` declaration per **1.5** can leave an unused import behind, triggering **4.2**

A pass only catches what was true when it ran. Resuming mid-cycle after a fix skips re-checking the sections that already passed — which is exactly where the fix's side effects land.

**Stop condition:** one full iteration with no failures, not "no failures found so far." A run that fixes 4 issues in iteration 1 and finds 0 in iteration 2 has converged after 2 iterations, not 1. Record the iteration count in the summary.

### Pass 1 — Machine checks

Run these before reading anything. Any hit is a failure to fix, not a judgment call.

```bash
FILE=src/components/uiable/.../component-name.tsx

# §2.2, §2.4, §12.1–12.3, §12.8 — styling and polish smells
grep -nE 'style=\{\{|#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}|rgba?\(|hsla?\(|oklch\(|console\.(log|warn|error)|\{/\*|TODO' "$FILE"

# §1.5, §9.7 — module-scope arrow helpers, reflexive memoization
grep -nE '^(export )?const [A-Za-z0-9_]+ = (\(|async \()|useMemo\(|useCallback\(' "$FILE"

# §1.3, §1.4 — type-only and data-only files beside the component
ls "$(dirname "$FILE")" | grep -E '^(types|data)\.tsx?$|\.data\.tsx?$'

# §3.13 — every <svg> in the file; confirm in Pass 2 each is its own function, not inline in a return
grep -n '<svg' "$FILE"

# §2.7 — raw px/rem lengths in bracket syntax; each has a scale equivalent to look up.
# Deliberately does not match percentages, calc(), unitless ratios, or arbitrary properties
# like [background-size:24px_24px] — those are legitimate bracket uses, not findings.
grep -noE '\[-?[0-9.]+(px|rem)\]' "$FILE"

# §2.10 — off-scale z-index (anything but z-0/10/20/30/40/50)
grep -noE '\bz-\[?[0-9]+\]?' "$FILE" | grep -vE ':z-(0|10|20|30|40|50)$'

# §4.4 — cn imported through the @/lib/utils re-export instead of directly
# from the "cn" package. This is backwards: flip it to `import { cn } from "cn"`.
grep -n 'cn.*from "@/lib/utils"' "$FILE"

# §2.6 — every margin AND space-x/y class; each hit is a sibling-spacing candidate to convert to
# gap in Pass 2. space-* compiles to margin on children, so it belongs in this grep, not outside it.
# (padding is not flagged here — p-*/px-*/py-* is normal internal spacing, not sibling spacing.
#  this only catches the margin-already-present case — a multi-child container with NO margin
#  and NO gap is still a violation, but that needs the JSX tree, so it's a Pass 2 check.)
grep -noE '\bm[trblxsey]?-[a-zA-Z0-9.\[\]]+|\bspace-[xy]-[a-zA-Z0-9.\[\]]+' "$FILE"

# §4.2, §5.4, §12.5–12.7 — types, unused imports, formatting
npm run type-check
npm run lint
npx prettier --check "$FILE"
```

Hex inside a `<path d="…">`, a `viewBox` or an image URL is geometry or a URL, not a colour — ignore those. Hex or `rgba()` inside `className`, or inside an SVG `fill`/`stroke`/`stopColor`, is a colour and is a finding under **2.4**. `useMemo`/`useCallback` hits are not automatic failures — each one must be justified under **9.7**. Every `<svg` hit is a manual check under **3.13** — the grep only finds the tag, it cannot tell whether it is already its own function.

### Pass 2 — Read-through

Read the file **once** and evaluate §1–§11 and §14 together, grouped by what is already on screen:

- **Top-to-bottom shape** → §1 (ordering: imports → types → data → helpers → component)
- **Imports block** → §4 (order, aliases, group comments)
- **Types and props** → §5
- **JSX structure** → §3 (primitives over raw HTML, composition, whole-hierarchy reuse), §8 (semantics, a11y), §10 (naming)
- **`className` strings** → §2 (layout, cleanup), §6 (dark mode), §7 (breakpoints), §11 (design tokens). For every container with ≥2 children, confirm `gap-*` is present (**2.6**) — not just where margin was flagged in Pass 1
- **Logic and handlers** → §9 (performance, memoization), §14 (readability)

### Pass 3 — Registry

Only if the component is a block. Otherwise mark §13 as ⏭️ N/A.

**Multi-file blocks.** If the block folder holds a `components/` subfolder, every file in it is in scope — run Pass 1 across all of them and Pass 2 on each. Report them as one review, attributing each failure to its own file.

```bash
for f in "$(dirname "$FILE")"/*.tsx "$(dirname "$FILE")"/components/*.tsx; do
  [ -e "$f" ] && echo "--- $f" && grep -nE '\{/\*|console\.(log|warn|error)' "$f"
done
```

### Pass 4 — Judgment

**The checklist is the floor, not the ceiling.** It holds what recurs across many blocks; it cannot anticipate what one specific block gets wrong. After working the numbered checks, look at the block as a whole and ask the question no check asks: *what is wrong here that nothing above named?*

Fix what you find. Three rules keep this from becoming either a rubber stamp or a rewrite:

1. **Fix it, do not merely note it.** An off-checklist finding is held to the same standard as a numbered one — it is repaired in this iteration and re-verified on the next. Treat it as a ❌ for convergence purposes: an iteration that fixed something has not converged.
2. **Record every one in the summary**, in its own section, with the same columns as a numbered fix — what changed, where, and why. A fix the report does not mention is indistinguishable from an unrequested edit.
3. **Do not add it to this file.** One occurrence is an observation, not a rule.

The same restraint that applies to the block applies here: stay inside the edit surface (**3.4**), and do not "improve" something that is merely not to your taste. An off-checklist finding is a defect — something broken, unsafe, misleading, or unmaintainable — not a preference.

**When an off-checklist finding earns a number.** Promote it into this checklist only when all three hold:

- it has appeared in **at least three separate blocks**, not three times in one file
- it is **mechanically checkable** — a grep, a DOM assertion, or a rule a reviewer can apply without re-deriving the reasoning
- it applies to **blocks generally**, not to one category of block

Anything short of that stays a judgment call. This checklist is already long, and every rule added to it is paid for by every future review — a check that fires once in fifty blocks costs more attention than it saves. The "Off-checklist fixes" section of past reports is the intake queue: when the same finding shows up in it for the third time, that is the signal to write the rule.

