## 1. File Structure & Ordering

Every component file follows one fixed order. Nothing is placed "wherever it landed".

```text
Imports
  ↓
Types / Interfaces
  ↓
Static Data / Constants
  ↓
Normal Helper Functions
  ↓
Main Component
  ↓  Component Hooks / State
  ↓  Component Arrow Functions / Callbacks
  ↓  JSX
```

- [ ] **1.1** The file follows the order above end to end — no type, constant, or helper stranded between other blocks
- [ ] **1.2** **Types directly below imports** — every type/interface declared in the file is grouped immediately after the last import, never interleaved with constants or functions
- [ ] **1.3** **No type-only file** — do not create a separate `types.ts` for types used by one component. Declare them in the component file
- [ ] **1.4** **No separate data file** — component-specific static data stays in the same file, declared after the types and before the helpers. Do not create `data.ts` / `*.data.ts` for data only this component reads
- [ ] **1.5** **Helpers above the component are `function` declarations** — never arrow assignments:

  ```tsx
  function formatName(name: string) { ... }        // ✅
  const formatName = (name: string) => { ... }     // ❌
  ```

- [ ] **1.6** **Arrow functions inside the component** — handlers and callbacks declared in the component body use arrow syntax (`const handleClick = () => { ... }`)

> **Exception:** a block large enough to warrant a `components/` subfolder may also carry sibling `types.ts` / `data.ts`. This is the only case where 1.3 and 1.4 do not apply, and it needs a real size justification, not convenience.

