## 2. Styling Correctness & Layout

- [ ] **2.1** All visual styling lives in Tailwind utility classes in `className`
- [ ] **2.2** No inline `style={{}}` — the one exception is a dynamic value Tailwind cannot express:

  ```tsx
  <div style={{ transform: `translateX(${offset}px)` }} />
  ```

- [ ] **2.3** Conditional classes use `cn()` from `@/lib/utils` — not string concatenation or template literals
- [ ] **2.4** No hardcoded colour values — use CSS variable tokens (`bg-primary`, `text-foreground`, `border-border`) or Tailwind palette colors (`bg-gray-100`, `text-slate-900`).

  **`rgb()`, `rgba()`, `hsl()` and `oklch()` are hardcoded colours too.** Hex is the obvious form and the one people remember to avoid; the function forms hide in arbitrary values and slip through every review — `drop-shadow-[0_0_12px_rgba(96,165,250,1)]`, `shadow-[0_20px_40px_rgba(0,0,0,0.5)]`, `bg-[radial-gradient(...,rgba(249,115,22,0.45),transparent)]`. A shadow or glow tuned to one specific theme is exactly what breaks when the buyer changes theirs (**11.6**, `block-audit` **7.1**). Prefer a token with an opacity modifier (`shadow-primary/20`, `bg-primary/10`).

  **This applies inside SVG too, where the value is a colour.** `fill`, `stroke`, `stopColor` and gradient stops are colours and follow this rule; `d`, `viewBox` and other geometry attributes are not colours and are exempt. A hand-built SVG whose gradient stops are six hardcoded oranges is a themed component in name only
- [ ] **2.5** **Grid/Flex first** — CSS Grid (`grid grid-cols-*`) for main structure, Flexbox (`flex flex-col/row`) for internal alignment
- [ ] **2.6** **Gap over margin/padding (CRITICAL)** — this is the **top-priority layout rule**: the moment a container holds two or more things — paragraphs, cards, `div`s, icons and text, anything at all — the space between them is managed with `gap-*` on that container. This applies by default, not only as a fix for margin already present in the source. Writing a new container with two children, reviewing one that already exists, extracting a sub-component per **3.9** — in every case, check the container: if it has ≥2 children and no `gap-*`, that is a violation, whether or not any child currently carries margin. Do not use `m-*`/`mt-*`/`ms-*`/`p-*`/`pt-*`/`ps-*` for sibling spacing unless `gap` genuinely cannot produce the design (e.g. spacing that must skip one specific child).

  **`space-x-*` and `space-y-*` are margin, and they are banned here for exactly the same reason.** They read like a gap utility, which is why they survive review, but they compile to `margin-top` / `margin-left` on every child except the first — the precise thing this rule exists to eliminate. They are also the most common way the rule gets bypassed without anyone writing the word "margin". `space-y-1` on a two-paragraph stack is a **2.6** violation, not a tidy alternative to it:

  ```tsx
  <div className="space-y-1">          {/* ❌ margin on children, wearing a gap costume */}
  <div className="flex flex-col gap-1"> {/* ✅ */}
  ```

  They carry two further defects that `gap` does not: the first-child exclusion silently breaks when children are conditionally rendered or reordered, and the injected margins collapse and interact with adjacent margins in ways `gap` never does.

  The fix is mechanical — apply it directly, do not just flag it. Two situations, same rule:

  ```tsx
  // ❌ Margin used for spacing
  <div>
    <p>First paragraph</p>
    <p className="mt-4">Second paragraph</p>
    <Card className="mt-6">...</Card>
  </div>

  // ❌ No margin either — but two children, no gap: still a violation
  <div className="flex flex-col">
    <Avatar />
    <span>Full Name</span>
  </div>

  // ✅ Both become the same shape — gap on the parent, no per-child spacing
  <div className="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <p>First paragraph</p>
    <p>Second paragraph</p>
    <Card className="mt-6">...</Card>  {/* different gap value: still needs its own wrapper, or an outer gap-6 */}
  </div>

  <div className="flex flex-col gap-2">
    <Avatar />
    <span>Full Name</span>
  </div>
  ```

  When the source used a **responsive** margin (`mt-4 md:mt-6`), carry the same breakpoints onto `gap` (`gap-4 md:gap-6`) rather than dropping the responsiveness — see **7.6**. If siblings in one parent need two different gap values, split them into their own grouping `div`s, each with its own `gap-*` — do not fall back to per-child margin to fake mixed spacing.

- [ ] **2.7** **Never put a raw `px` or `rem` length in bracket syntax.** Use the scale. In v4 the scale includes **bare numeric utilities** — `max-w-200`, `w-220`, `gap-18` resolve against `--spacing` (`4px`) and are first-class, not arbitrary — so the 4px-based scale is effectively complete and a bracketed pixel value is nearly always a value someone did not look up:

  | ❌ Hardcoded length | ✅ Scale equivalent |
  | :------------------ | :------------------ |
  | `min-h-[200px]` | `min-h-50` — 200 ÷ 4 |
  | `w-[1px]` / `h-[1px]` | `w-px` / `h-px` |
  | `rounded-[2rem]` | `rounded-4xl` |
  | `text-[8px]` / `text-[13px]` | `text-xs` / `text-sm` — and see **11.5**, nothing renders below 12px |
  | `opacity-[0.15]` | `opacity-15` |
  | `p-[13px]` | `p-3` — snap to nearest, per **11.6** |

  When a length genuinely has no scale equivalent, **snap to the nearest one rather than bracketing it.** `text-[2.65rem]` sits between `text-4xl` and `text-5xl`; pick one. This is **11.6** applied to dimensions instead of colour — a block that matches a mockup to the pixel while stepping outside the scale is worse than one that is 2px off and consistent, because the buyer's spacing and radius scales are theirs to change and hardcoded lengths will not follow.

  **Bracket syntax stays correct** for values the scale cannot express by design, and these are not findings: percentages (`h-[300%]`, `w-[55%]`, `top-[5%]`), `calc()`, arbitrary properties (`[background-size:24px_24px]`), gradients, masks, `aspect-[4/3]`, and unitless ratios (`leading-[0.9]`). The test is whether the scale *has* an answer — not whether brackets were used
- [ ] **2.8** **Clean CSS — every class in the string must actually do something.** Typos (`overflow-hidde`) are the easy case. The expensive case is a **valid Tailwind class that has no effect in its context** — it survives every linter, reads as intentional, and accumulates fast in migrated and junior-authored blocks. Delete each one; do not leave it "in case".

  **A step that is not on the scale is the typo that survives review.** `text-slate-505` reads as completely plausible — it is only wrong because the palette runs `50, 100, 200 … 900, 950` and `505` is not a rung. Unlike `overflow-hidde`, nothing looks misspelled, so it passes every skim. Check the *value* as well as the utility: colour steps against that scale, `z-*` against **2.10**, and spacing against **2.7**. Any off-scale value generates no CSS at all, so the element silently keeps whatever it inherited.

  Work this table against every `className` in the file:

  | # | Dead pattern | Example | Why it does nothing |
  | :- | :----------- | :------ | :------------------ |
  | 1 | Flex/grid child props, non-flex/grid parent | `items-center` on a child of a `block` div | `align-items` only applies inside a flex/grid container |
  | 2 | `gap-*` on a non-flex/grid element | `<div className="gap-4">` with no `flex` | `gap` needs a flex, grid or multi-column container |
  | 3 | `flex-col` without `flex` | `className="flex-col"` | `flex-direction` needs a flex container |
  | 4 | Offsets without positioning | `top-0`, `inset-0` on a `static` element | offsets only apply to positioned elements |
  | 5 | `z-*` on a static, non-flex/grid child | `z-10` on a plain static div | `z-index` applies to positioned elements and flex/grid items only |
  | 6 | Border color with no border width | `border-red-500` alone | preflight sets `border-width: 0` — nothing renders |
  | 7 | `h-full` with no parent height | `h-full` inside an auto-height div | `height: 100%` of `auto` resolves to `auto` |
  | 8 | `truncate` with no width constraint | `truncate` on a flex child with no `min-w-0` | the element never narrows, so it never truncates (**3.3** in `block-audit`) |
  | 9 | Duplicate property, last wins | `p-4 p-6`, `text-sm text-lg` | the earlier one is overwritten and dead |
  | 10 | Responsive variant identical to base | `text-sm md:text-sm` | the variant changes nothing at any width |
  | 11 | `dark:` identical to base | `bg-card dark:bg-card` | same value in both themes |
  | 12 | `duration-*` / `ease-*` with no `transition-*` | `duration-300` alone | nothing is transitioning |
  | 13 | `transition-*` with nothing that changes | `transition-colors` and no `hover:`/`focus:`/state variant | a transition with no trigger |
  | 14 | `group-*` with no `group` ancestor | `group-hover:opacity-100` | the variant never matches |
  | 15 | `peer-*` with no `peer` sibling | `peer-checked:block` | same |
  | 16 | `focus:*` on a non-focusable element | `focus:ring-2` on a plain div | it can never receive focus |
  | 17 | Explicit Tailwind defaults | `flex-row`, `justify-start`, `items-stretch`, `opacity-100`, `font-normal`, `static` | already the default — only keep when overriding a breakpoint or a variant |
  | 18 | `aspect-*` with a fixed height | `aspect-video h-64` | the explicit height wins; the ratio is dead (**2.11**) |
  | 19 | `shrink-0` / `grow` on a non-flex child | `shrink-0` in a `block` parent | flex-item properties need a flex parent |
  | 20 | `space-x-*` alongside `gap-*` | `flex gap-4 space-x-2` | `gap` already spaces them, and `space-*` adds margins that violate **2.6** |
  | 21 | Text properties on an element with no text | `leading-7` on a wrapper holding only divs | nothing inherits it usefully |
  | 22 | `divide-*` with fewer than two children | `divide-y` on a single-child div | no divider is ever drawn |
  | 23 | `overflow-*` on a replaced element | `overflow-hidden` on an `<img>` or `<video>` | replaced elements have no overflowing children — the clip belongs on the wrapper, and putting it here usually means someone was fighting a `rounded-*` corner |
  | 24 | Inherited text property every child overrides | `text-white/70` + `group-hover:text-white` on a div whose children each set their own `text-*` | nothing inherits it, so neither the colour nor its hover transition can ever be seen |
  | 25 | `grid-flow-*-dense` with fully explicit placement | `grid-flow-row-dense` where every child sets `col-start`/`row-start` | dense packing only reflows auto-placed items; there are none |
  | 26 | Resetting a property preflight already zeroes | `mb-0`, `mt-0`, `p-0`, `gap-0` on an element with nothing to remove | preflight and the grid/flex defaults are already `0` — only keep it when it overrides a component default or a variant |
  | 27 | `max-w-*` wider than a constraining ancestor | `max-w-200` on a `<p>` inside a `max-w-105` wrapper | the ancestor caps it at 420px, so the 800px limit can never apply. Very common after a copy-paste between blocks with different wrapper widths — and it hides the fact that nobody chose the real value |
  | 28 | `gap-*` on a container with fewer than two children | `<div className="grid gap-2"><Input /></div>` | `gap` is space *between* children; with one child there is no between. Usually a wrapper that used to hold a `<Label>` and lost it — which is also **8.11** |
  | 29 | A class from the framework the block was migrated from | `f-w-500`, `d-flex`, `text-muted`, `btn`, `row`, `col-md-6` | not Tailwind, generates nothing, and survives every linter because it is a syntactically valid class name. Berry, Mantis and Saascable are Bootstrap-descended, so this residue is specific to migrated blocks and always inert |

  Three that are **not** findings: `w-full` on a block element is redundant but harmless and often intentional at a breakpoint; a shorthand/longhand pair like `p-4 px-6` is a normal override, not dead code; and class **order** is never reviewed — `prettier-plugin-tailwindcss` sorts it automatically, including inside `cn()` and `cva()`.

  Rendered verification is `block-audit` **7.7**, which proves patterns 1–8 and 14–19 against computed styles rather than by reading. **Every class removed must be listed in the report** — a silent cleanup is indistinguishable from a careless one.
- [ ] **2.9** Border radius uses Tailwind (`rounded-sm`, `rounded-lg`, `rounded-xl`, `rounded-full`) — arbitrary values only when non-standard
- [ ] **2.10** **z-index follows the established stacking scale** — `z-0`, `z-10`, `z-20`, `z-30`, `z-40`, `z-50`. A new layer snaps to the next rung on that scale; do not introduce an off-scale value (`z-1`, `z-15`, `z-[5]`) to squeeze between two existing layers — bump the whole stack instead
- [ ] **2.11** **`aspect-*` over a fixed height for responsive media** — an image or video container that must hold a ratio uses `aspect-video` / `aspect-square` / `aspect-[4/3]`, not a fixed `h-*` that breaks the ratio at other widths
- [ ] **2.12** **Long or dynamic text is bounded** — a label, title, or description whose length isn't controlled by the author uses `truncate` (one line) or `line-clamp-*` (multiple lines) so it cannot blow out the layout. Skip this for text that is always short and known ahead of time
- [ ] **2.13** **Standard block anatomy** — this is the shape to reach for when **creating** a new block, and what to check for when reviewing one: a `<section>` root (semantic, also satisfies **8.6**) holding one centered, width-bounded content wrapper:

  ```tsx
  export default function FeatureSection() {
    return (
      <section className="bg-background py-16 sm:py-24">
        <div className="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
          {/* block content */}
        </div>
      </section>
    )
  }
  ```

  - **Root:** `<section>`, not a bare `<div>` — every top-level block in the registry does this
  - **Content wrapper:** `mx-auto max-w-<value> px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8` — centers the content and keeps its edges off the viewport edge on mobile. The `max-w-*` value is design-dependent (`max-w-7xl` for wide sections, a bare-numeric value like `max-w-200` for a narrower centered block per **2.7**) — match the design, don't default to one number everywhere
  - **Vertical rhythm:** the section's own `py-*` grows at `sm:` — `py-16 sm:py-24`, `py-20 sm:py-25`, `py-24 sm:py-32` are all in use. Pick from that range; don't invent an unrelated value

- [ ] **2.14** **No Tailwind v3 utility names left in a v4 codebase.** Some v3 names were removed in v4 and generate nothing; worse, a group of them **still exist in v4 but mean something one step larger**, so a migrated block renders a heavier shadow, a rounder corner or a stronger blur than its author chose — with no error and nothing visibly broken to notice.

  | v3 | v4 | Failure mode |
  | :- | :- | :----------- |
  | `shadow-sm` | `shadow-xs` | **silent** — `shadow-sm` in v4 is v3's `shadow` |
  | `shadow` | `shadow-sm` | **silent** — one step heavier |
  | `rounded-sm` | `rounded-xs` | **silent** — one step rounder |
  | `rounded` | `rounded-sm` | **silent** |
  | `blur-sm` / `blur` | `blur-xs` / `blur-sm` | **silent** — same shift |
  | `drop-shadow-sm` / `drop-shadow` | `drop-shadow-xs` / `drop-shadow-sm` | **silent** |
  | `backdrop-blur-sm` / `backdrop-blur` | `backdrop-blur-xs` / `backdrop-blur-sm` | **silent** |
  | `bg-gradient-to-*` | `bg-linear-to-*` | renamed |
  | `outline-none` | `outline-hidden` | renamed |
  | `ring` | `ring-3` | v4's bare `ring` is 1px, not 3px |
  | `flex-shrink-*` / `flex-grow-*` | `shrink-*` / `grow-*` | renamed |
  | `overflow-ellipsis` | `text-ellipsis` | renamed |
  | `bg-opacity-*`, `text-opacity-*`, `border-opacity-*` | the `/` modifier — `bg-black/50` | removed |

  The silent half is what makes this worth a check of its own: the renamed utilities simply stop working and get noticed, while the shifted ones keep rendering and quietly drift the whole block off the design.

  Two stages, because a single pattern cannot do this — `-` is not a word character to `grep`, so `\bshadow\b` matches inside `shadow-md` and buries the real hits. Split the class strings into tokens first, then match each token **exactly** with `-x`:

  ```bash
  grep -oE '[a-zA-Z0-9:/_.-]+' "$FILE" | grep -xE \
    '([a-z0-9-]+:)*(shadow|shadow-sm|rounded|rounded-sm|blur|blur-sm|drop-shadow|drop-shadow-sm|backdrop-blur|backdrop-blur-sm|ring|outline-none|overflow-ellipsis|flex-(shrink|grow)-[0-9]+|(bg|text|border)-opacity-[0-9]+|bg-gradient-to-[a-z]+)' \
    | sort | uniq -c

  # then locate each name the first stage reported
  grep -n 'shadow-sm' "$FILE"
  ```

  The variant prefix in the pattern is what catches `dark:shadow-sm` and `md:bg-gradient-to-r`; the exact match is what lets `shadow-md`, `rounded-2xl`, `blur-3xl`, `ring-2` and `shadow-blue-500/20` through untouched.

  **A file mixing both vocabularies is the strongest signal** — `shadow-sm` beside `shadow-xs`, or `bg-gradient-to-tr` beside `bg-linear-to-br`, means a partial migration, so assume every shifted name in that file is still carrying its v3 intent.

  While here, prefer `size-*` over a matching `h-N w-N` pair (`size-4`, not `h-4 w-4`). Not a defect — both compile — but it is the current idiom and halves the class string

- [ ] **2.15** **`framer-motion` is scoped to what actually animates** — wrap the smallest element that needs motion, not the whole section or a static parent. Animated props stay on **transform**/**opacity** (`x`, `y`, `scale`, `opacity`), same constraint as **9.5** — do not animate `width`/`height`/`top`/`left` through `motion.div` either.

  **`repeat: Infinity` needs a reason, and "it looks nice" is not one.** A never-ending animation never lets the compositor idle: it holds a raf loop and keeps a layer alive for as long as the page is open, on every device that loads it. One looping block on a page is a battery cost the buyer did not choose; several are a visibly janky page. Prefer an animation triggered by entry (`whileInView` with `once: true`) or by interaction (`whileHover`). When a loop genuinely is the design — a marquee, a ticker — it still must pause under `prefers-reduced-motion` (`block-audit` **2.6**) and must not be the block's only motion

