## 4. Import Organization

Import order is not a judgment call here — it is enforced by `perfectionist/sort-imports` at **error** severity in `eslint.config.mjs`. That means **4.1** and **4.5** are already covered by the `npm run lint` step in Pass 1, and `npm run lint -- --fix` will insert the group comments and reorder the block for you. Do not hand-place the comments; run the fixer, then confirm the result against the table. If the table below and `eslint.config.mjs` ever disagree, the config wins and this table is the thing to correct.

- [ ] **4.1** Groups appear in this order, each preceded by its comment. Order, group membership, and comment text all come from the `groups` / `customGroups` arrays in `eslint.config.mjs`:

  |  #  | Group          | Matches                                                                    | Comment              |
  | :-: | :------------- | :------------------------------------------------------------------------- | :------------------- |
  |  1  | `"use client"` | a directive, not an import — perfectionist does not sort or comment it      | —                    |
  |  2  | `react`        | `react`, `react/*`                                                          | —                    |
  |  3  | `next`         | `next`, `next/*`                                                            | `// next`            |
  |  4  | `shadcn`       | `@/components/ui/*`                                                         | `// shadcn`          |
  |  5  | `external`     | node_modules packages                                                       | `// third-party`     |
  |  6  | `project`      | `@/data/*`, any `@/*` except `@/types/*`, relative paths not containing `types` | `// project-imports` |
  |  7  | `assets`       | `lucide-react`, `@tabler/icons-react` — those two packages only             | `// assets`          |
  |  8  | `types`        | `@/types/*`, relative paths containing `types`                              | `// types`           |
  |  9  | `constant`     | `@/constant`, `@/constants`, `@/constant(s)/*`, relative `*constant(s)`     | `// constant`        |
  | 10  | `unknown`      | anything matching none of the above                                         | —                    |

  Note two things this table previously got wrong: **`next` carries a `// next` comment** — it is not comment-less the way `react` is — and **`constant` is a real group** that was missing altogether.

  The `types` group is matched on the **import path**, not on `import type` syntax. `import type { Foo } from "@/lib/foo"` sorts into `project`, because only the path is tested. Only `@/types/*` and relative paths with `types` in the specifier reach the `types` group.

- [ ] **4.2** No unused imports — but note `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars` is set to **`warn`**, not `error`, so `npm run lint` exits 0 with unused imports still in the file. Read the warning list in Pass 1; a zero exit code is not this check passing
- [ ] **4.3** No circular import dependencies
- [ ] **4.4** Path aliases use the `@/` prefix (`@/components/ui/button`, `@/lib/utils`) — **except `cn`**, which is always imported directly from the third-party `cn` package (`import { cn } from "cn"`, grouped under `// third-party`), never through the `@/lib/utils` re-export. `src/lib/utils.ts` only re-exports `cn` for backward compatibility; importing from there instead of `"cn"` is a violation, not a fix — flip it the other way if found. This is the dominant convention codebase-wide (150+ files import `cn` directly; treat any file using `@/lib/utils` for it as the outlier to correct). The two forms also sort differently, which makes the violation visible: `"cn"` is a package and lands in `external` under `// third-party`, while `@/lib/utils` matches `^@/(?!types/)` and lands in `project` under `// project-imports`
- [ ] **4.5** A single blank line separates each group so the group comments stay visible — `newlinesBetween: 1` in the rule config enforces exactly one

