## 8. Accessibility

- [ ] **8.1** All interactive elements are keyboard-accessible (Shadcn primitives handle this by default)
- [ ] **8.2** Images have descriptive `alt` text — not empty, not generic
- [ ] **8.3** Icon-only buttons have `aria-label`
- [ ] **8.4** Color is never the sole means of conveying information
- [ ] **8.5** Focus states are visible (Shadcn primitives ship `focus-visible:` styles)
- [ ] **8.6** Semantic HTML where appropriate — `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<article>`, `<aside>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, and for quoted content `<blockquote>` with `<cite>` for the attribution. A testimonial is the canonical case: the quote is a `<blockquote>` and the name is a `<cite>`, not two `<div>`s with CSS `content: open-quote` doing the work of the markup.

  **`<br>` is not a layout tool.** It puts layout into the content: the break is real text content, assistive tech may still announce it, and the "fix" for the next screen size is another variant class. Two different misuses, two different replacements — decide which one you are looking at before changing anything:

  | `<br>` is being used to… | Replace with |
  | :----------------------- | :----------- |
  | **Divide content** — separate two groups, or open vertical space between them | **`Separator`** from `@/components/ui/separator`. It is the real element for a thematic break (`role="separator"`), it is themeable, and it is what **3.10** means by preferring a component over raw markup. If the intent was only space and not a visible division, that is `gap-*` on the parent (**2.6**) |
  | **Wrap a line of text** — `<br className="block sm:hidden" />` to break a heading at one breakpoint | **`text-balance`** or `text-pretty`, letting the browser choose the break; or wrap the segment in `<span className="block sm:inline">` so the *element* changes flow. A `Separator` here would render a visible rule through the middle of the heading |

  `<br>` survives in exactly one case: where the break is part of the content itself — a postal address, a line of verse, a preserved-format snippet. `<hr>` is likewise always `Separator`
- [ ] **8.7** Heading hierarchy is logical — single `<h1>`, sequential `<h2>` → `<h3>`.

  **Anything that reads as a heading must be a heading element.** A visually dominant line marked up as `<div className="text-4xl font-black">` or `<p className="text-3xl">` is invisible to crawlers, to screen-reader heading navigation, and to the document outline — the three audiences headings exist for. A block whose design has four headings and whose markup has none is not "heading-free"; it has four broken headings. Blocks start at `<h2>` (`block-audit` **5.1**), and the level comes from structure while the size comes from classes — `<h2 className="text-sm">` is correct whenever the structure calls for it.

  The inverse is equally wrong: do not reach for `<h5>` because the text should be small. That is what produced `<h5>` sitting under no `<h2>` at all, which skips four levels and breaks the outline in the other direction
- [ ] **8.8** Links have discernible text — not just "click here"
- [ ] **8.9** **Decorative content is hidden from assistive tech** — not just icons. An icon beside its own text label carries `aria-hidden="true"` so screen readers do not announce it twice; icons that _are_ the only label fall under **8.3** instead.

  **Decorative *text* is the case that gets missed.** A giant watermark numeral, a repeated background word, a letter-by-letter animated logo, an `&` or `/` used as a visual divider — these are real text nodes and a screen reader reads every one of them, interleaved into the content. A three-panel section with `01` / `02` / `03` blurred behind each panel announces those numbers in the middle of the prose, and nothing about the markup hints that it is decoration:

  ```tsx
  <span className="absolute text-[180px] blur-xs">01</span>                    {/* ❌ announced */}
  <span aria-hidden="true" className="absolute text-[180px] blur-xs">01</span> {/* ✅ */}
  ```

  The test is whether removing the element would cost a non-sighted reader anything. If the answer is no, it is `aria-hidden="true"`. If the numeral is genuinely meaningful — a real step number in a process — then it belongs *in* the heading text, not as a decorative layer behind it

- [ ] **8.10** **Anything that moves on its own can be paused.** WCAG 2.2.2 is explicit: content that auto-updates, scrolls or animates for more than five seconds needs a mechanism to pause, stop or hide it. An autoplaying carousel is the usual offender, and `onMouseEnter={() => plugin.stop()}` does **not** satisfy it — that is a mouse-only affordance, so keyboard and touch users have no way to stop the movement at all, and those are exactly the users a moving target hurts most.

  What actually satisfies it, in rough order of preference:

  - **A real control** — `CarouselPrevious` / `CarouselNext` plus a visible pause toggle. Manual controls also fix the separate problem that an autoplay-only carousel is unreachable by keyboard
  - **`stopOnInteraction`** so any interaction halts autoplay permanently rather than resuming and moving the target again
  - **Pause on focus as well as hover** — `onFocus`/`onBlur` alongside `onMouseEnter`/`onMouseLeave`, so a keyboard user tabbing into a slide stops it
  - **Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`** by not autoplaying at all for those users (`block-audit` **2.6**)

  The same rule covers marquees, tickers, auto-rotating hero banners and looping counters — anything a reader has to race. It pairs with **2.15**: a loop needs a reason to exist, and once it exists it needs a way out

- [ ] **8.11** **A block with form controls is a real form.** Inputs and a submit button sitting in `<div>`s look identical to a working form and behave nothing like one. Wrap them in `<form>` (or Shadcn `Form`), and check all four of these — sign-up, sign-in, contact, newsletter and checkout blocks fail at least one of them almost every time:

  | Requirement | What breaks without it |
  | :---------- | :--------------------- |
  | A real `<form>` element | `required` **never fires** — it is inert outside a form. Enter does not submit. Password managers and browser autofill do not recognise the block as a form at all, which for a sign-up block defeats its purpose |
  | A `<Label htmlFor>` per control | A `placeholder` is not a label: it vanishes the moment the user types, is announced inconsistently, and leaves the field nameless to assistive tech. Use `Label` from `@/components/ui/label`, or `sr-only` on it when the design wants placeholder-only styling. Rendered counterpart: `block-audit` **6.10** |
  | `autoComplete` on every field | `given-name`, `family-name`, `email`, `new-password` on sign-up, `current-password` on sign-in. Without them autofill misfires or does nothing, and a password manager will not offer to save credentials |
  | `type="submit"` on the submit button, `type="button"` on every other | An unmarked button inside a form submits it, so a secondary action silently submits instead of doing its own job |

  **Consent checkboxes ship unchecked.** A terms-of-service or marketing-opt-in box carrying `defaultChecked` is not a styling choice — pre-ticked consent is explicitly invalid under GDPR (Recital 32 requires a clear affirmative act), and shipping one hands that exposure to every buyer who installs the block. `defaultChecked` is fine on a genuine convenience toggle such as "remember me"; it is never correct on a box that records agreement or permission.

  Two further signs of a form copied from another block: a control whose `id` describes a different field than its label (`id="remember"` on a terms checkbox), and a `<Label>` carrying `peer-*` variants with no `peer` on the control beside it — the second is dead CSS under **2.8** pattern 15

