  `radius` (`--radius: 0.5rem`) backs the standard `rounded-*` scale (**2.9**) and is not picked by hex.

  **Only when no token above fits** — a status color, a chart hue, a one-off accent — fall back to Tailwind v4's built-in palette. Every family below runs the same 11-step scale (`50` lightest → `950` darkest), so `bg-<family>-<step>` and `text-<family>-<step>` always resolve:

  | Family    | Typical use            | Family    | Typical use      |
  | :-------- | :--------------------- | :-------- | :--------------- |
  | `slate`   | cool neutral / dark UI | `orange`  | warm accent      |
  | `gray`    | neutral text/surfaces  | `amber`   | warning (alt)    |
  | `zinc`    | neutral, slightly warm | `yellow`  | warning          |
  | `neutral` | true neutral           | `lime`    | success (bright) |
  | `stone`   | warm neutral           | `green`   | success          |
  | `red`     | danger / destructive   | `emerald` | success (alt)    |
  | —         | —                      | `teal`    | info (alt)       |
  |           |                        | `cyan`    | info             |
  |           |                        | `sky`     | info (alt)       |
  |           |                        | `blue`    | primary-adjacent |
  |           |                        | `indigo`  | accent           |
  |           |                        | `violet`  | accent           |
  |           |                        | `purple`  | accent           |
  |           |                        | `fuchsia` | accent           |
  |           |                        | `pink`    | accent           |
  |           |                        | `rose`    | danger (alt)     |

  Pick the step by weight, not by eye: `50`–`200` for subtle backgrounds and `/10`-style tints (**11.3**), `500`–`600` for solid fills and default text, `700`–`900` for high-contrast text on light backgrounds or dark-mode surfaces.

  **Consume a token as `var(--token)` — never wrap it in `hsl()`.** The tokens above hold complete colour values (`#155dfc`), so `hsl(var(--chart-1))` resolves to `hsl(#155dfc)`, which is invalid CSS. It throws no error and logs no warning: the property is simply dropped and the element renders unstyled or black. This is the single most common way a themed chart or gradient silently loses its colour, because `hsl(var(--x))` was the **correct** form under the older HSL-triplet convention and gets carried over from v3 code, shadcn v3 snippets and any LLM trained on them.

  ```tsx
  color: "hsl(var(--chart-1))"   // ❌ invalid — silently dropped
  color: "var(--chart-1)"        // ✅
  ```

  Grep for it: `grep -n 'hsl(var(--' "$FILE"`. Any hit is a failure, not a judgement call. And when a chart or SVG declares a token this way, check whether the value is even reaching the element — a `ChartConfig` colour that is never read because the mark hardcodes its own `stroke` is dead configuration under **12.4**.

- [ ] **11.2** Solid variants use high-contrast text (`text-white`)
- [ ] **11.3** Light variants use low-alpha background + colorized text — `bg-green-600/10 text-green-700`
- [ ] **11.4** Outline variants include `bg-transparent` when needed to block inherited backgrounds
- [ ] **11.5** Typography follows the `globals.css` base sizes — do not fight the global `text-base: 0.875rem`.

  **No rendered text below 12px.** `text-[8px]` and `text-[10px]` are not a design choice, they are a legibility failure: below roughly 12px, text is unreadable for a large share of users, fails at arm's length on a phone, and is the first thing to break when a buyer's font stack has different metrics than ours. If a label must look small, reach for `text-xs` with `tracking-wide` and `uppercase` — that reads as small without being unreadable. Bracket sizes below the scale (`text-[8px]`) are a finding under **2.7** as well, since the scale deliberately stops where it stops
- [ ] **11.6** **Closest token wins** — when a source design uses a color with no matching token in `globals.css` or the Tailwind v4 palette, snap to the nearest existing token. Never introduce a new hardcoded value (`bg-[#2878d4]`) to match the original exactly; theme consistency outranks pixel fidelity

