## 13. Registry Compliance (Blocks Only)

⏭️ N/A if the component is not a block.

- [ ] **13.1** Block lives at `src/components/uiable/blocks/<category>/<block-name>/<block-name>.tsx`
- [ ] **13.2** `src/category-counts.json` is updated (for new blocks)
- [ ] **13.3** Registry regenerated via `npm run registry:generate` — **never hand-edit `registry.json`**
- [ ] **13.4** Default export follows **3.5** and **10.2**
- [ ] **13.5** Self-contained — a consumer installs the block and nothing else. Everything it needs is either declared in the block file itself (per **1.3** and **1.4**) or imported from `@/components/ui/*`. No imports from other blocks, no shared local helpers
- [ ] **13.6** A flat `blocks/<category>/<block-name>/` is the only shape the registry generator understands — do not introduce nested category groups
- [ ] **13.7** **No real destinations — every `href` is `#`.** A registry block is a template, not a page; it cannot know where a consumer's links should point, and shipping a real destination sends their users somewhere the block author picked. Replace every one with `#`: absolute URLs (`https://…`), internal routes (`/pricing`), `mailto:`, `tel:`, and the `href` on `next/link` and on any anchor inside `<Button asChild>` alike.

  ```bash
  grep -n 'href=' "$FILE" | grep -v 'href="#"'
  ```

  Anything that survives both greps is a real destination to replace — including `href={someVar}`, where the destination is simply hidden behind a variable.

  **Keep the attribute; only change its value.** Deleting `href` instead of setting it to `#` trades a placeholder problem for an accessibility one — an `<a>` with no `href` is not focusable and is not announced as a link (**8.8**). `href="#"` stays focusable, stays announced, and is the correct placeholder.

  **One exception:** an in-page anchor pointing at an `id` *inside this same block* (`href="#pricing-faq"`) is a real, working link and stays. The rule is about destinations outside the block, not about every `#`-prefixed value.

- [ ] **13.8** **Zero-config install — nothing outside the block and `@/components/ui/*`.** These blocks are sold; a buyer installs one into *their* project and it must render correctly with no further setup. Anything the block silently depends on is a support ticket on day one. Specifically, none of:

  - a keyframe or `@utility` defined in **our** `globals.css` (`animate-marquee`, `animate-shimmer` and friends) — if the block animates, the keyframe travels with it or the animation goes
  - a **Tailwind plugin** we happen to have installed
  - a **specific font** — a block may set weight and size, never a family the buyer has not loaded
  - a **CSS variable outside the standard token set** in §11.1 — those exist in the buyer's theme; anything we invented does not

  ```bash
  grep -nE 'animate-[a-z]|font-\[|font-(sans|serif|mono)|var\(--' "$FILE" \
    | grep -vE 'animate-(spin|ping|pulse|bounce)\b'
  ```

  Each surviving hit is a manual check, not an automatic failure: confirm the animation is one Tailwind ships, and that every `var(--…)` names a token from the §11.1 table rather than something only our `globals.css` defines. Rendered verification is `block-audit` **7.2**, which catches what a grep cannot — a dependency arriving through a Shadcn primitive instead of through this file.

- [ ] **13.9** **Placeholder content is licensed for resale and professionally written.** Everything shipped in a block is redistributed to every buyer, so placeholder content carries the same licensing burden as the code:

  - **No real brand marks** — no Google, Stripe or Nike logo in a "trusted by" row. Trademark use in a commercial product is not fair use, and logo walls are where this reliably goes wrong
  - **No identifiable real people** — testimonial headshots and team photos must come from a source licensed for commercial redistribution, not a web search. Name and photo together make it worse, not better
  - **Images from a redistributable source**, with the license recorded in the PR. A hotlinked URL is also an availability risk: it breaks the buyer's block when it 404s
  - **No `lorem ipsum`.** On a product sold on its design, the copy *is* part of the design — plausible, domain-appropriate placeholder text is the difference between a block that looks finished and one that looks like a draft
  - **Proofread it.** Spelling, grammar and punctuation in placeholder copy ship to every buyer and appear in every gallery screenshot. `Continuos Grow` for "Continuous Growth", or a missing space in `growth.By embracing`, costs nothing to fix and is the single cheapest signal of carelessness a prospective buyer can spot. Read the rendered copy once, out of the JSX, before signing off — typos are invisible while you are reading class strings

  ```bash
  grep -niE 'lorem ipsum|dolor sit|placeholder\.com|via\.placeholder|unsplash\.com|John Doe|Jane Doe' "$FILE"
  ```

