## 14. Code Readability & Maintainability

- [ ] **14.1** **Extract complex conditions** into named variables — `const isSubmitDisabled = !isValid || isSubmitting`
- [ ] **14.2** **No magic numbers** — arbitrary numeric values become constants with descriptive names
- [ ] **14.3** **Focused functions** — functions and event handlers stay short and single-responsibility
- [ ] **14.4** **Destructuring** is used for props and objects
- [ ] **14.5** **No deep nesting** — replace nested ternaries with early returns or extracted render functions
- [ ] **14.6** **Self-documenting** — descriptive, unambiguous names remove the need for comments
- [ ] **14.7** **Never assume the shape of data a buyer will replace.** The static array in a block is placeholder data; the first thing a buyer does is swap it for theirs (`block-audit` **7.4**). Any code that indexes into it positionally will crash on data that is perfectly valid but shaped differently:

  ```tsx
  name.split(" ")[1].charAt(0)   // ❌ TypeError on "Cher", "Prince", "Madonna"
  ```

  A single-word name makes `[1]` `undefined` and `.charAt(0)` throws — taking down the whole block, not just the initials. The same applies to `items[0]` on a possibly-empty array, `.split()[2]`, and any non-null assertion on a value that came from data.

  Derive defensively, or do not derive at all:

  ```tsx
  const initials = name.split(" ").map((w) => w[0]).slice(0, 2).join("")   // ✅
  ```

  Better still for a field like this: put `initials` in the data object. A value the block cannot compute safely from placeholder content is a value the data should carry

